@startsalt
!include https://raw.githubusercontent.com/proveo-ca/identity/main/proveo.puml
' purpose: the resolved layout of `proveo init`'s credential screen — one masked field per credential, checked when sbx holds it
' SHIPPED 2026-09-16 in 9a3c724. This was a PROPOSAL until the stage landed;
' the drawing below is now the surface an operator actually meets.
' covers: cmd/proveo/init_credentials.go
' covers: internal/choiceui/choiceui.go
' covers: apps/cli/public/cli/install.sh (run_init)
'   the field's rules and the verify screen _spec/cmd/proveo/init-credential-entry.puml
'   what is still UNBUILT behind it ....... _spec/_plans/init-credential-provisioning.puml
'   the form this reuses ....... _spec/internal/choiceui/wireframe.puml
'   where a secret rests ....... _spec/_plans/secret-custody.puml
'   what the agent may hold .... _spec/_paradigms/credential-boundary.puml
'   the store's two names ...... _spec/internal/sbx/credential-path.puml
'
' ── WHAT THIS EXISTS TO FIX ────────────────────────────────────────────────
' Measured 2026-09-16 on a fedora host with no credential of any kind: every
' def reached its login prompt and exited there, ~10s per run, and the operator
' read it as a crash. proveo said the true thing twice in the same output —
' "running anyway; the agent will handle login", then "the sbx backend cannot
' complete a login" — which is a contradiction a reader resolves by disbelieving
' both. The Keychain that holds the macOS answer is darwin-only by design, so
' there is no equivalent surface on linux to point at.
'
' `sbx setup` is not that surface either: it imports what the host already
' exports and prompts for nothing, so an operator holding a key in their head
' has nowhere to type it.
'
' ── THE ONE RULE THESE SCREENS OBEY ────────────────────────────────────────
' A secret is never drawn, never echoed, never an argv word, and never written
' to a file proveo owns. The form collects the DECISION; the value is read after
' the form exits, with echo off, and goes straight to `sbx secret set` on stdin.
'
' SUPERSEDED BY WHAT SHIPPED, and kept because the reasoning was sound and the
' answer still had to satisfy it. The original argument was that key entry must
' be a SEPARATE SCREEN rather than a row, because a TUI row accepting a secret
' would be the first place proveo held key material in its own process longer
' than a write. The shipped form puts the field IN the list anyway — and meets
' the same rule a different way: `choiceui.Row.Masked` never renders what was
' typed, the value lives only in the form's redraw buffer, and it is zeroed on
' accept and on cancel alike. `Form.typing` takes the keyboard while the cursor
' is on a field, so a `q` inside a pasted key is a character rather than a
' cancel — the hazard a row-based field actually had, which is not the one the
' original argument named.
'
' ── A CREDENTIAL IS NOT A PRECONDITION, AND THIS SCREEN DOES NOT PRETEND IT IS
' Operator's question, 2026-09-16: why are credentials necessary? They are not.
' An agent launches without one and shows its own login, and `/login` is the
' AGENT's own command — proveo neither drives it nor needs a row for it. It
' persists because the def declared what to keep: claudecode's manifest lists
' `.claude` and the `.claude.json` at the home root, and `proveo_sync_config`
' carries exactly that set out at teardown and back in at seed. The manifest
' comment says it in one line — "docker persists it with the whole-home bind;
' sbx has to be told."
'
' So this screen provisions the credential an operator holds as a STRING — an
' api key, or a token minted on the host — and stays out of the flow the agent
' already owns. It offers; it does not gate the run.
'
' Not to be confused with `proveo_sync_state`, which walks /proc/mounts for
' /dev-backed mounts under the agent home. That one is the docker-volume path
' and finds nothing under an sbx home — it carries volume state, never a login.
'
' ── WHERE THE OPERATOR MEETS THIS SCREEN: THE INSTALL, NOT A MANUAL ────────
' The intended flow is install → init, and the installer already does it:
' apps/cli/public/cli/install.sh run_init() hands the fresh binary the second
' half of the install. So this screen is not something an operator has to know
' to type; it is the last thing they see after one `curl | bash`.
'
' That fixes the two constraints it must satisfy:
'   A TERMINAL IS NOT GIVEN. `curl … | bash` leaves stdin attached to the
'     SCRIPT. The installer opens /dev/tty and passes it to init, so the form
'     draws; where there is no controlling terminal it runs `init --yes`, and
'     the credential stage must then REPORT and provision nothing rather than
'     block a Dockerfile or a CI step.
'   A FAILED HOST IS NOT A FAILED INSTALL. init already exits non-zero when the
'     host cannot run a sandbox — missing KVM, a user not in the kvm group —
'     and the installer treats that as a host verdict. A credential the operator
'     declines is not even that: it is a normal answer, so it exits zero.
'
' ── THE LIST IS FILTERED BY THE AGENT, NOT BY THE REGISTRY ─────────────────
' Operator's rule: show what the relevant agent can actually use, subscription
' first. Two shapes follow, because two kinds of def exist:
'   NARROW def — claudecode, codex, cursor. One vendor, one plan. Its own
'                subscription is the top row, named as the thing it is (`/login`
'                inside the agent, or the env var that carries the same token),
'                and the api key for that same vendor sits under it.
'   BROAD def  — opencode, cecli. Many providers, none privileged. The short
'                list: providers a def runs on, plus any whose key is already on
'                this host. The other two dozen the registry knows are reachable
'                through `sbx secret set` and named in the footer.
'
' ── A LIST OF INPUTS, NOT A LIST OF CHOICES ────────────────────────────────
' Operator's direction, 2026-09-16: each entry is a labelled INPUT. Empty when
' the sbx store has nothing for it, checkmarked when it does, and overrideable
' either way — typing into a checked field replaces what is stored.
'
' That is a better fit than an options row, because the question is never "which
' of these three" — it is "here is the credential, or nothing". A radio row asks
' the operator to first declare an intent and only then be given somewhere to
' type it; the field IS the intent. It also makes the screen a readable
' inventory: what this host can spend, and what it cannot, in one column.
'
' THE FIELD IS MASKED AND STAYS MASKED. It renders as dots while typing, never
' echoes, never reaches argv, and is never written to a file proveo owns — the
' same rule the separate hidden prompt existed to guarantee. It supersedes that
' prompt for keys: init-credential-entry.puml's screen 2 is now this field, and
' that file keeps the verify screen and the rules the field must obey.
'
' A CHECKED FIELD SHOWS NO VALUE, EVER. The store can say a name is present; it
' cannot hand the value back, and proveo would not print it if it could. The
' check means "this host can spend it", and the placeholder says what replacing
' it would do.
'
' EVERY FIELD CARRIES A PLACEHOLDER, INCLUDING THE EMPTY ONE — "empty — type a
' key". A blank box is ambiguous in the one way that matters here: it reads as
' either "nothing is set" or "something is set and hidden", and those have
' opposite consequences for the next run. The placeholder states which, and what
' typing into it would do.
'
' THE AMBER ONE IS ALSO A ONE-KEY ACTION. A key already in this host's env needs
' no retyping — Enter imports it into the store, which is the whole of the P1
' additive migration for that provider, done without the operator finding the
' value again.

title The last screen of the install — one input per credential, checked when sbx holds it

{
{+
  {
    <&terminal> proveo SOLUTIONS | proveo/sandbox: portable, safe AI coding agents
  }
  ==credentials for claudecode — a narrow def: one vendor, subscription first
  {
    host: | linux/amd64 · sbx 0.42.0 · KVM present
    store: | sbx secret store — host-wide, outlives every run, the agent never holds it
    without one: | <color:#9a9a9a>the agent still launches and shows its own login; /login is the agent's, not proveo's
  }
  ~~
  {
    . | . | ———— subscription ———— | .
    ▸ claudecode | <color:#00BAC6><&check></color> | "stored — type to replace              " | <color:#9a9a9a>`claude setup-token`, or just /login inside the agent
    . | . | ———— api keys ———— | .
    anthropic | <color:#00BAC6><&check></color> | "stored — type to replace              " | <color:#9a9a9a>stored as `anthropic` and ANTHROPIC_API_KEY
    openai | . | "empty — type a key                    " | <color:#9a9a9a>sbx holds nothing for it; leaving it empty changes nothing
    google | <color:#E8A33D><&warning></color> | "this host's env only — Enter to import" | <color:#E8A33D>GEMINI_API_KEY is set here; init moves it into the store
  }
  --
  ↑↓ item · type to enter · enter accept · esc cancel — empty fields change nothing
  ..
  every other provider: `sbx secret set <service>` · one sbx does not know: `sbx secret set-custom --host <api-host> --env <VAR>`
}
{
{^"1 · a BROAD def — opencode, cecli — same list, more items"
  . | ———— subscription ————
  opencode | <&check> | "stored          "
  . | ———— api keys ————
  anthropic | <&check> | "stored          "
  openai | . | "empty — type a key"
  google | . | "empty — type a key"
  .
  No vendor is privileged, so the first
  item is the def's own plan and the rest
  are what it can route to: def-backed,
  plus any key already on this host. The
  other two dozen stay in the footer.
}
{^"2 · three states one field can be in"
  anthropic | <color:#00BAC6><&check></color> | "stored — type to replace"
  openai | . | "empty — type a key      "
  google | <color:#E8A33D><&warning></color> | "env only — Enter to import"
  .
  Every field says what it holds and what
  typing would do — a blank box says
  neither. Checked: the store answers for
  it. Empty: it does not, and the agent
  will meet its own login. Amber: the key
  is in this host's env — it works today,
  but one `unset` empties it, so it is not
  drawn as a pass, and Enter alone moves
  it into the store without retyping.
}
{^"3 · overriding a stored one — typing is the whole gesture"
  cursor | <color:#00BAC6><&check></color> | "••••••••••••          "
  .
  A checked field is not locked. The check
  says the store can answer; typing
  replaces what it answers with. Nothing
  is written until the form is accepted,
  and the old value is never shown — the
  store cannot hand it back, and proveo
  would not print it if it could.
}
{^"4 · post-install with no tty — report, never block"
  . | Setting up the sbx backend…
  . | secrets
  . | anthropic — sbx store: anthropic
  . | opencode — this host's env only
  . | <color:#9a9a9a>not a terminal — run `proveo init` from a shell to provision
  .
  `curl | bash` in CI reaches init through
  `--yes`, with no /dev/tty to open. Every
  path still REPORTS; only the asking needs
  a terminal, and declining exits zero.
}
{^"5 · the two kinds never share one store id"
  claudecode | <&check> | "stored   "
  anthropic | <&check> | "stored   "
  .
  A subscription stores as `claudecode`,
  the key as `anthropic`. One id cannot
  carry both: a proxy attaching an OAuth
  token into an x-api-key header answers
  401 to a credential that is good. Where
  a def is NAMED for its provider, its
  subscription takes `<service>-sub`.
}
}
}
@endsalt
